.TH BLUETUI 1 "August 2026" "bluetui 0.2.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bluetui \- nmtui-style terminal UI for managing Bluetooth devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bluetui
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bluetui
is a lightweight terminal user interface for managing Bluetooth on Linux,
modelled after NetworkManager's
.BR nmtui (1).
It talks to BlueZ over D-Bus (via sd-bus from libsystemd) and draws its
interface with newt. No configuration, no daemon of its own: it runs anywhere
you have a terminal and a running
.BR bluetoothd (8).
.PP
The device list updates live from BlueZ signals. Connected devices are marked
with
.BR * ,
paired ones with
.BR + ;
connected and paired devices sort to the top, the rest by signal strength.
If more than one Bluetooth adapter is present, a picker is offered at startup.
.PP
bluetui registers itself as a BlueZ pairing agent
.RB ( org.bluez.Agent1 ,
.SM KeyboardDisplay
capabilities), so pairing dialogs — PIN entry, passkey entry, passkey
confirmation — are shown and answered inside the program.
.PP
Connect, disconnect and pair run asynchronously: the interface stays live and
shows the operation in progress. A pairing dialog left unanswered for more
than 180 seconds ends with a timeout.
.PP
On exit — including
.BR SIGINT " (Ctrl-C) and " SIGTERM
— bluetui stops the discovery it started and unregisters its pairing agent.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Up / Down
Move in the device list.
.TP
.B Enter
Connect / disconnect the selected device.
.TP
.B p
Pair the selected device.
.TP
.B t
Toggle trust on the selected device.
.TP
.B o
Toggle showing only paired devices.
.TP
.B d
Show device details: battery level, device type, service UUIDs.
.TP
.B r
Remove the selected device (asks for confirmation).
.TP
.B s
Start / stop scanning.
.TP
.B /
Filter the device list by name.
.TP
.B Tab
Move between the list and the buttons.
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc ", " F10
Quit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BLUETUI_LOG
When set to a writable file path, every D-Bus call, its result and any error
text are appended to that file. Unset (the default), logging is a no-op.
.SH NOTES
The device list is capped at 256 entries; a "list full" row is shown when it
overflows. Device details show at most 8 service UUIDs.
.PP
bluetoothd must be running, and the user should be in the
.B bluetooth
group; root is not required.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bluetoothctl (1),
.BR nmtui (1),
.BR bluetoothd (8)
